# General

- Read referenced files fully — never use limit/offset to read partial files
- Prefer early returns to reduce nesting
- Handle errors explicitly; never swallow exceptions silently

# Naming Conventions

- Component files: PascalCase (`Button.tsx`, `UserProfile.tsx`)
- All other files: camelCase (`utils.ts`, `userService.ts`)
- Variables, functions, methods: camelCase
- Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE
- Types and interfaces: PascalCase

# TypeScript

- Avoid `any` — provide explicit types or use generics
- Prefer `type` over `interface` — use for object shapes, unions, and utility types
- Prefer `string[]` over `Array<string>`
- Always use arrow function syntax
- If a function has more than 2 parameters, extract a named type:

```typescript
// ✅ 2 or fewer params — inline is fine
const updateUser = ({ id, name }: { id: string; name: string }) => { ... }

// ✅ 3+ params — extract the type
type CreateUserParams = {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  email: string;
};
const createUser = ({ id, name, email }: CreateUserParams) => { ... }

// ❌ Avoid positional params
const createUser = (id: string, name: string, email: string) => { ... }
```

# React & Components

- Components are **dumb** — they render UI and nothing else. No business logic, no data fetching, no complex state.
- **Never prop drill.** All shared state and logic lives in a Context provider.
- Each page/feature gets its own Context that wraps the page. Components inside consume it via a custom hook.
- The `return` statement of a component should be purely rendering — readable at a glance.
- Use functional components with hooks — never class components
- Keep components small and single-responsibility
- Use `useMemo` for expensive calculations, `useCallback` for callbacks passed as props

```
app/
  dashboard/
    page.tsx              ← wraps content in <DashboardProvider>
    DashboardContext.tsx   ← context + provider + hook (useDashboard)
    StatCard.tsx           ← dumb component, consumes useDashboard()
    ActivityFeed.tsx       ← dumb component, consumes useDashboard()
```

```tsx
// DashboardContext.tsx — all logic lives here
type DashboardContextValue = {
  stats: Stat[];
  isLoading: boolean;
  refresh: () => void;
};

const DashboardContext = createContext<DashboardContextValue | null>(null);

export const useDashboard = () => {
  const ctx = useContext(DashboardContext);
  if (!ctx) throw new Error("useDashboard must be used within DashboardProvider");
  return ctx;
};

export const DashboardProvider = ({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) => {
  const [stats, setStats] = useState<Stat[]>([]);
  const [isLoading, setIsLoading] = useState(true);

  const refresh = () => { /* fetch logic */ };

  useEffect(() => { refresh(); }, []);

  return (
    <DashboardContext.Provider value={{ stats, isLoading, refresh }}>
      {children}
    </DashboardContext.Provider>
  );
};

// page.tsx — just wraps in the provider
export default const DashboardPage = () => (
  <DashboardProvider>
    <StatCard />
    <ActivityFeed />
  </DashboardProvider>
);

// StatCard.tsx — dumb component, zero props needed
export const StatCard = () => {
  const { stats, isLoading } = useDashboard();
  return isLoading ? <Spinner /> : <div>{stats.length} items</div>;
};

// ❌ Never do this — prop drilling
const Page = () => {
  const [stats, setStats] = useState([]);
  return <StatCard stats={stats} onRefresh={() => setStats([])} />;
};
```

# Imports

- Use absolute imports via configured path aliases
- Group: external libs → internal modules → relative imports → types
- Remove unused imports

# Error Handling

```typescript
// ❌ Silent failure
try {
	await fetchData();
} catch (e) {}

// ✅ Handle and surface errors
try {
	await fetchData();
} catch (e) {
	console.error('Failed to fetch data', e);
	throw e;
}
```
